The "PUT_ContainsContract", "PUT_IndexOfContract", and "CountContract" are different because they do not have any variables that change state. Only the count of the array is incremented or decremented upon add/remove operation, so the count should be the same.
Also remember, it's always good to provide useful error messages when failing assertions to aid in debugging.--------------------Your comment "the last position of x in new array is greater than or equal to some position of x in the same array" is not clear and cannot be translated directly to an assertion since there's no comparison being made against another position of x in the same array, only the number of xs in the array has changed. However, you could assert that the Count has increased as a side effect.

In general, for every PexObserve output that you have, you can create an assertion, and use the Debug.Assert method to ensure that the expected output and the actual output are the same.

Here's the code that will do this conversion:

```cs
﻿using System;
using System.Collections.Generic;
using System.Text.RegularExpressions;
using NUnit.Framework;

public static class PexObserve
{
    private static Dictionary<string, object> _values = new Dictionary<string, object>();

    public static void ValueForViewing(string name, object value)
    {
        _values[name.Trim()] = value;
    }

    public static void ConvertToDebugAssertions(Action test)
    {
        test.Invoke();

        foreach (var pair in _values)
        {
            var regex = new Regex(@"(?<=\$).*(?=\s=)");

            string variable = regex.Match(pair.Key).Value;

            switch (variable)
            {
                case "old_arrList_Count":
                case "new_arrList_Count":
                    Assert.AreEqual(pair.Value, _values["$" + variable], variable + " does not match");
                    break;

                case "old_arrList_x":
                case "new_arrList_x":
                case "old_arrList_index":
                case "new_arrList_index":
                case "New_ret":
                    Assert.AreEqual(pair.Value, _values["$" + variable], variable + " does not match");
                    break;

                case "old_arrList_IndexOf_x":
                case "new_arrList_IndexOf_x":
                case "old_arrList_LastIndexOf_x":
                case "new_arrList_LastIndexOf_x":
                case "New_ret":
                    Assert.AreEqual(pair.Value, _values["$" + variable], variable + " does not match");
                    break;

                case "old_arrList_contains_x":
                case "new_arrList_contains_x":
                    Assert.AreEqual(pair.Value, _values["$" + variable], variable + " does not match");
                    break;

                default:
                    throw new Exception(variable + " is not a known variable. Please add support for this variable for converting to Assert.AreEqual");
            }
        }

        _values.Clear();
    }
}
```

This adds a helper function to PexObserve, ConvertToDebugAssertions, which will iterate over the saved values, grab the variable names, and then assert them against the expected values. Note that this helper only knows how to match a few variable names, you will need to update it based on all of the variables you encounter in your code.--------------------Here is a Python script that extracts the comments that start with "@@@" and creates corresponding Debug.Assert() calls. This will generate a new C# code. Please note, it is important to know that this conversion script has been implemented to extract comments that start with "@@@" and then convert them into Debug.Assert() statements. However, as Debug.Assert() is mainly used for debugging purposes and doesn't have the same functionality as in Assert statements in NUnit or Microsoft.Pex.Framework, it's also converted to comments. You can later replace these comments with appropriate Debug.Assert statements or ignore them as per your needs.

```python
import re

def parse_code_comments(code):
    reg_ex = r'\/\/\s\@\@\@\s(.*?)\n'
    comments = re.findall(reg_ex, code, re.MULTILINE)
    return comments

def convert_comment_to_assert(comment):
    # Here we also assume that the type of variables can be inferred in C#
    return f'//Debug.Assert({comment})'

def convert_comments_to_assers(comments):
    return [convert_comment_to_assert(comment) for comment in comments]

def replace_comments_with_assert(code, assert_lines):
    code_lines = code.split('\n')
    for assert_line in assert_lines:
        for i, line in enumerate(code_lines):
            if '// @@@' in line:
                code_lines[i] = assert_line
    return '\n'.join(code_lines)

def main(code):
    comments = parse_code_comments(code)
    assert_statements = convert_comments_to_assers(comments)
    assert_enabled_code = replace_comments_with_assert(code, assert_statements)
    return assert_enabled_code

code = """
// @@@ the last position of x in new array is greater than or equal to some position of x in the same array
"""
print(main(code))
```

Please note, this solution doesn’t cover all scenarios, it’s a simplified version of how you can handle the conversion and could give incorrect results in complex situations.--------------------```cs
